### Option 1: Simple For Loop (Recommended for beginners)
This is the most straightforward way to calculate the sequence.

```csharp
using System;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        int n = 10;
        int firstNum = 0;
        int secondNum = 1;

        Console.WriteLine($"First {n} Fibonacci numbers:");

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(firstNum);

            // Calculate the next number in the sequence
            int nextNum = firstNum + secondNum;

            // Shift values for the next iteration
            firstNum = secondNum;
            secondNum = nextNum;
        }
    }
}
```

### Option 2: Using an Iterator (Professional Approach)
In a real-world project, you usually want to separate the **logic** (calculating the numbers) from the **output** (printing them). Using `IEnumerable` allows you to generate the numbers on demand.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        // Take the first 10 numbers from the generator and print them
        foreach (int num in GetFibonacci().Take(10))
        {
            Console.WriteLine(num);
        }
    }

    // This method generates an infinite sequence of Fibonacci numbers
